# LLC vs S Corporation

A comparison of two of the most common entity types for small businesses, focusing on liability protection, tax treatment, flexibility, and operational considerations.

## Liability Protection

Both LLCs and S Corps provide full limited liability protection for owners. Members/shareholders are generally not personally liable for business debts and obligations.

## Tax Treatment

| Feature | LLC | S Corporation |
|---------|-----|---------------|
| Default Taxation | Pass-through | Pass-through |
| Tax Election Options | Can elect corporate taxation (Form 8832) | Fixed pass-through (cannot elect C Corp) |
| Self-Employment Tax | Paid on all net earnings | Paid only on salary |
| Profit Allocation | Flexible | Must be proportional to ownership |
| Distribution Flexibility | Flexible timing and amounts | Must be proportional to ownership |

## Formation and Compliance

- **LLC**: Articles of Organization, Operating Agreement, EIN. Moderate compliance.
- **S Corp**: Articles of Incorporation, Bylaws, Form 2553 (S Election). Higher compliance due to corporate formalities.

## When to Choose LLC

- Desire for flexible profit allocation
- Simpler formation and administration
- No need for S Corp tax optimization
- Prefer less formal governance requirements

## When to Choose S Corp

- Significant earnings where self-employment tax optimization matters
- Need for corporate credibility
- Planning for equity compensation (stock options)
- Want to minimize FICA taxes on distributions

